WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.858
Picture the owner of Fern Bakery.

2
00:00:04.858 --> 00:00:07.147
A customer wants a refund of 40.00.

3
00:00:07.147 --> 00:00:14.079
She finds the payment on her dashboard, a web page that lists the day's card payments, and presses Refund.

4
00:00:14.079 --> 00:00:15.911
The answer: no such payment.

5
00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:17.758
But the payment is there.

6
00:00:17.758 --> 00:00:24.184
The program that records payments has it, under a long number, nineteen digits, ending in 4017.

7
00:00:24.184 --> 00:00:29.325
It sent that number to the dashboard, and neither program reported an error.

8
00:00:29.325 --> 00:00:31.219
So where did the payment go?

9
00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:36.658
The two programs are written in different languages, so they agree on text.

10
00:00:36.658 --> 00:00:45.644
The payment program writes the payment as JSON, a plain text format every language can read: each name in quotes, then its value.

11
00:00:45.644 --> 00:00:47.176
Here it is, 116 bytes.

12
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:51.273
The dashboard runs JavaScript, the language web pages run.

13
00:00:51.273 --> 00:00:57.115
It reads the text, and turns every number into a JavaScript number, which is a double.

14
00:00:57.115 --> 00:01:02.346
And here's what the dashboard shows: the same number, except it ends in 4000.

15
00:01:02.346 --> 00:01:04.384
Seventeen off, and no warning.

16
00:01:04.533 --> 00:01:08.712
A refund for that number finds nothing, or someone else's payment.

17
00:01:08.712 --> 00:01:10.421
That's the missing payment.

18
00:01:10.421 --> 00:01:14.409
Why would a computer change a number nobody asked it to change?

19
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:15.893
Think about scales.

20
00:01:15.893 --> 00:01:18.612
A kitchen scale weighs flour to the gram.

21
00:01:18.612 --> 00:01:24.049
The weighbridge that weighs lorries moves in bigger steps, say 20 kilos at a time.

22
00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:24.911
Nobody minds.

23
00:01:24.911 --> 00:01:27.232
Twenty kilos on a lorry is nothing.

24
00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:29.165
A double works the same way.

25
00:01:29.165 --> 00:01:32.120
It has room for 53 binary digits, and no more.

26
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:35.588
Small numbers get exact steps, like the kitchen scale.

27
00:01:35.588 --> 00:01:38.671
Big numbers get big steps, like the weighbridge.

28
00:01:38.671 --> 00:01:42.589
And a number between two steps is snapped to the nearest one.

29
00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:48.114
Up to 2 to the 53, about nine quadrillion, every whole number has its own step.

30
00:01:48.114 --> 00:01:50.634
Just above it, the steps are 2 apart.

31
00:01:50.634 --> 00:01:53.291
Here are four numbers around that edge.

32
00:01:53.291 --> 00:01:55.266
Which ones come back changed?

33
00:01:55.266 --> 00:01:56.356
Pause and guess.

34
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:00.895
Only one.

35
00:02:00.895 --> 00:02:06.455
2 to the 53, plus 1, has no step of its own, so it lands on 2 to the 53.

36
00:02:06.455 --> 00:02:10.780
The even numbers on either side have steps, and survive.

37
00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:18.822
Our payment number is far bigger, about two hundred times bigger, so its steps are 256 apart.

38
00:02:18.822 --> 00:02:21.633
The nearest step to 4017 is 4000.

39
00:02:21.633 --> 00:02:23.592
There's the missing 17.

40
00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:28.103
So the scale picture holds, but it breaks in one place that matters.

41
00:02:28.103 --> 00:02:31.252
A lorry's weight is an amount, and close is fine.

42
00:02:31.252 --> 00:02:32.986
A payment number is a name.

43
00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:35.685
Off by 17 is a different payment, or none.

44
00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:39.194
You might be thinking JSON should have complained.

45
00:02:39.194 --> 00:02:39.799
It can't.

46
00:02:39.799 --> 00:02:43.563
The document that defines JSON sets no size for numbers.

47
00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:49.075
It only warns that whole numbers up to 2 to the 53, minus 1, work in every reader.

48
00:02:49.075 --> 00:02:56.133
Twitter hit this with the numbers of its posts, and added a second field holding the same number as text.

49
00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:57.689
So here's the rule.

50
00:02:57.689 --> 00:03:01.050
A big number in JSON is safe only as a string.

51
00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.243
Send 64-bit numbers in quotes.

52
00:03:03.243 --> 00:03:11.793
In quotes, the dashboard keeps all 19 digits as text, and a Java program reads it straight back into a 64-bit number.

53
00:03:11.967 --> 00:03:14.566
And numbers are only the first gap.

54
00:03:14.566 --> 00:03:20.879
JSON can't tell a whole number from a fraction: is 40.0 an exact amount, or a double?

55
00:03:20.879 --> 00:03:27.637
It has no type for raw bytes, so a 3 KB signature travels as Base64 text and grows to 4 KB.

56
00:03:27.637 --> 00:03:32.687
And nothing in a JSON document says what shape the record should be.

57
00:03:32.687 --> 00:03:35.509
Every reader guesses, in its own code.

58
00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.555
So, where did the payment go?

59
00:03:37.555 --> 00:03:38.066
Nowhere.

60
00:03:38.066 --> 00:03:42.159
The number was sent right, and read into a box too small for it.

61
00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:44.334
Two programs never share a number.

62
00:03:44.334 --> 00:03:48.555
One writes bytes, and the other decides for itself what they mean.

63
00:03:48.733 --> 00:03:49.916
One quick check.

64
00:03:49.916 --> 00:03:55.460
The next payment's number ends in 4100, and it's sent bare, without quotes.

65
00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.604
What does the dashboard show?

66
00:03:57.604 --> 00:03:58.787
Pause and think.

67
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.039
It ends in 4000 too.

68
00:04:04.039 --> 00:04:07.710
4100 is nearer to 4000 than to 4256.

69
00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:11.687
Two payments, one number on the screen.

70
00:04:11.687 --> 00:04:14.848
Unless you send them in quotes.
